A||||15||skitime||Starter kill switch - Which wire do I cut?||||1||
Z||000000||skitime||11-11-2000||09:49 PM||||I installed a remote door & trunk lock system (Crimestoppers)that also has a feature to prevent starting the car without the remote.  It requires cutting the wire that turns the starter from the ignition switch and putting a relay in. I don't know which wire to cut. Does anyone know which wire under the drivers dash is the correct one and exactly wire the wire is? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||11-11-2000||09:52 PM||||Take off the plactic cover under the steering colum and you should find a heavy guage yellow wire. That is your wire.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||11-11-2000||09:56 PM||||I looked under the plate and see a thick yellow wire coming out of a blue connector is this the one?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||11-11-2000||10:04 PM||||To be honest, I am unsure about it comming from a blue connector, but I will run out and look real quick. It should be a little bigger then alot of the other wires, if not bigger then any others. I will check quickly and get back to you.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">    <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||11-11-2000||10:10 PM||||If you're car has a manual trans, the yellow wire goes to the clutch pedal switch, and leaves as a purple wire. You can cut either one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||11-11-2000||10:11 PM||||Mine is an auto point of view. From my view on the 86 GT, it is the thickest guage wire under there. It is yellow, tword the right (passenger) side of the steering colum. It plugs into a relay under the dash. It is a black connecter on mine, also has a smaller guage black wire w/white strip next to it, and above it there is a yellow wire w/black strip, all plugged into the relay. 3 wires. You want the very heave guage solid yellow wire going into the relay. <p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 11-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||11-11-2000||10:18 PM||||This is an automatic 88 GT. The only thick yellow wire I see is coming from a blue connector that also has a thick pink wire and a thinner pink wire coming out of it. I dont see any relay with a thick yellow wire. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||11-11-2000||10:29 PM||||Maybe other years are different, but I dont see anything like what you are saying. Could you tell me a little more like where it goes to and comes from, to the best of you visability. And the location under the dash.||||reg||1
Z||000008||skitime||11-11-2000||10:35 PM||||I am viewing the blue connector from the passenger side of the steering column. It is directly above to column, I cannot see what it is plugged into but the wires disappear into the wiring harness. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||11-11-2000||10:43 PM||||Okay, Try looking from the underside of the dash. See if you can see it now. I probally should have been more specific before. I meant the right side, but under the dash. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||skitime||11-11-2000||10:48 PM||||Seanh, I have been looking from the underside of the dash on the right side of the steering column.  Thanks for the time, I appreciate it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||11-11-2000||11:08 PM||||Im at a loss then. Maybe someone with an 88 like yours may be able to help. I cant see anything like that on my car. Maybe that is your wire. The only way to check would be hooking a volt meter to it. It should show positive 12 volts when cranking only. When you get a wire that does this, you've probally found it. I would tend to think maybe you have the right one, but I am not absoulutly sure. Check if you have a volt meter. Hook the positive lead to the wire and ground the other lead and start the car. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||RaeQwan||11-12-2000||12:05 AM||||OGRE! HELP!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwancfa.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||StuGood||11-12-2000||06:11 PM||||The fat yellow wire is probably the one you want to cut, as Seanh said.  Think the blue connector shell skitime is referring to goes to the ignition switch (the pink wire in that same connector tends to confirm this).<P>Believe the relay Seanh is talking about is the trunk release relay.  If skitime's car has no electric trunk release (or if it's nonfunctional because someone removed the relay), that would explain why skitime can't find a relay there.<P>See if you can get a length of the suspect (big fat yellow) wire pulled out away from the other wires. Lay a toy compass on the suspect wire, then bump the starter.  If the compass needle jumps, that's the one (you'll have to pull it out of the harness some, to splice the relay in anyway).  Or, as Seanh suggests, if you're pretty sure about the wire, go ahead and scrape off enough insulation that you can poke a voltmeter probe there, and check it that way.<P>By the way, on manual transmission cars, there's a clutch start switch (fat yellow wire in; and fat purple wire out) under the dash.  On automatics, the neutral start switch is back in the trans.  That's why you don't see any purple (start) wire under the dash, for autos.          ||||reg||3
Z||000014||skitime||11-12-2000||09:56 PM||||StuGood thanks for the info.  My car does have a remote trunk release but no rear defroster and is an automatic.  I like your compass test idea, I never tried that. I will probe the thick yellow wire but aren't a lot of wires electrically charged when the key is turned on?||||reg||1
Z||000015||StuGood||11-12-2000||10:55 PM||||Yeah, but some wires are hot even if the ignition switch is off!  If you're really concerned about it, disconnect the battery until you get the insulation scraped off.<P>I know very little about car alarms.  But I think the gist of what you're trying to do right now, when you cut the wire (the wire you're trying to locate and cut, and splice the relay into), the starter should be disabled.<P>So, if it were me, I'd probably disconnect the negative cable.  Pull the fat yellow wire out as far as practical.  Cut the yellow wire with diagonal cutters.  Strip some insulation from the cut ends.  Tape them up with electrical tape so they won't short.  Reconnect battery.  Put key into ignition and try to start the car.  You should get no cranking.<P>Assuming all of the above happens, disconnect battery again.  Connect the cut ends to relay (or what ever the destructions  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> say to do with them).<P>BTW, the compass thing may or may not work; I wouldn't run out and get one just for this.  If you have one handy (from your kids, etc.), try it.  Let me know if it works  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||3
